# Security Policy

Fernlet handles sensitive personal health data — period tracking, mental health context, intimate journal entries, photos of real people. Security issues in this codebase have real potential for personal harm, not just technical inconvenience. Please read this document before reporting.

---

## Supported Versions

| Version | Supported |
|---|---|
| Latest release | ✅ |
| Previous minor | ✅ (critical fixes only) |
| Older | ❌ |

---

## Reporting a Vulnerability

**Do not open a public GitHub issue for security vulnerabilities.**

Report privately via GitHub's [Security Advisories](../../security/advisories/new) feature. This creates an encrypted channel between you and the maintainers and keeps details out of public view until a fix is shipped.

If you are unable to use GitHub Security Advisories, email **security@fernlet.com** with the subject line `[SECURITY]`. PGP key available on request.

### What to include

- A clear description of the vulnerability and its potential impact
- Steps to reproduce or a proof-of-concept (even a rough one is helpful)
- Which data category is at risk (see priority tiers below)
- The iOS version, device model, and Fernlet version where you observed it

### What to expect

- **Acknowledgement within 48 hours**
- **Initial triage within 5 business days**
- We will keep you updated on progress and coordinate a disclosure timeline with you
- We credit researchers by name in release notes unless you prefer to remain anonymous

